Cell[TextData[{
 "Statement: a vertex list ",
 StyleBox["e", "TI"],
 "0, \[Ellipsis], ",
 StyleBox["ek", "TI"],
 " is a chain when it is nonempty, its elements are distinct events of ",
 StyleBox["g", "TI"],
 ", and ",
 StyleBox["e", "TI"],
 Cell[BoxData[
  SubscriptBox["", 
   RowBox[{"*", "i", "*"}]]], "InlineFormula",ExpressionUUID->
  "41037cfb-0494-4ac4-918a-b78e8f85466e"],
 " \[Precedes] ",
 StyleBox["e", "TI"],
 Cell[BoxData[
  SubscriptBox["", 
   RowBox[{"*", "i", "*", 
    RowBox[{"+", "1"}]}]]], "InlineFormula",ExpressionUUID->
  "2f9576ff-8f52-4e22-9bfd-fa5c14cfe079"],
 " for every ",
 StyleBox["i", "TI"],
 ": a directed path of ",
 StyleBox["g", "TI"],
 " leads from each event to the next."
}], "Notes",ExpressionUUID->"d2fd0a25-5824-4d4b-a375-e924be827bbf"],

Cell["\<\
Since the causal order is transitive, a chain is a totally ordered set of \
events listed in causal order: any two of its events are comparable, and the \
earlier one comes first. The consecutive events need not be adjacent, so a \
chain may skip events that lie between them; the chain predicates above it in \
the hierarchy forbid that. A single event is a chain; the empty list is not.\
\>", "Notes",ExpressionUUID->"166fe541-df55-4d02-99c1-0d6067eb9570"],

Cell["\<\
The chain predicates form a hierarchy, each condition stronger than the next:\
\
\>", "Notes",ExpressionUUID->"59e4eee2-b087-4091-a6f0-400d226a4c99"],

